{"step_index":135,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-12T08:40:40Z","thinking":"**Investigating Master Password Behavior**\n\nThe user describes a desired behavior where entering the master password persists across site refreshes, but the observed behavior requires re-entry. The issue likely stems from the browser's extension architecture and how it handles session storage within Manifest V3. This might be a consequence of the new architecture. Need to determine how to establish persistent storage.\n\n\n**Identifying Session Storage Persistence**\n\nThe background service worker's inactivity causes session data loss. Browser-provided `session` storage appears to solve this as it persists until the browser closes. It's designed to hold data in memory, encrypted by the browser, and available to the service worker and popup.\n\nStoring the derived session key in `session` storage seems viable, potentially allowing the vault to stay unlocked until the user explicitly locks it or closes the browser. This aligns with the desired persistent unlock behavior.\n\n\n**Analyzing Password Data Persistence**\n\nThe vault might be locking due to background worker idling, causing loss of the session key and resulting in the \"Locked\" screen. Alternatively, there might be an issue with how the saved items are being managed. Inspecting the `saveStoredItem` function reveals potential issues with item storage during the refresh process. The local storage might be the problem.\n\n\n**Evaluating Key Derivation & Transfer**\n\nThe `loadDecryptedItems()` function uses a master key derived by `deriveMasterKey`. The background script receives this key via messaging. However, this raises a concern. `CryptoKey` objects, particularly those with `extractable: false`, cannot reliably be transferred through browser messaging due to serialization limitations in Manifest V3.\n\nThis means the session key is lost when the service worker becomes inactive, and the vault locks on refresh. Exporting the key material, either as raw bytes or storing the derived key using another method like in-browser `session` storage, is crucial for persistence.\n\n\n**Implementing Session Key Persistence**\n\nNow considering a strategy for key persistence using browser `session` storage. The session key will be exported as raw bytes, base64 encoded, and stored. When needed, the key can then be imported from the storage. This design ensures the vault remains unlocked throughout a browser session. It avoids on-disk storage of the key and avoids needing the master password again.\n\n**Addressing Localhost-Extension Interaction**\n\nThe user's issue with password saving on localhost indicates a problem in data sharing between the extension and the local website. The standalone web application has its own storage. The extension's storage is separate. Investigating the bookmark icon's save behavior reveals the likely cause.\n\n\n**Unifying Password Storage Origins**\n\nLocalhost and the extension are distinct origins, leading to data silos. The localhost web app's IndexedDB storage isn't accessible to the extension. The extension's storage (`chrome.storage.local`) is also isolated from the web app.\n\nTo solve this, the extension should be the central vault. It can open a full-page dashboard to eliminate duplication. This dashboard, within the extension, utilizes the same `chrome.storage.local`.\n\nAll data saved in the extension is immediately available in the dashboard, and vice-versa, providing a unified experience.\n\n\n**Integrating Localhost Data into Extension**\n\nThe user's request for importing existing localhost data suggests the need for data migration. Offering a 1-click import, sync, or export/import functionality would enhance usability. Alternatively, making the extension's vault mimic the full web app can simplify data management. This approach unifies password storage. The extension now acts as the primary vault.\n\n\n**Implementing Session Key Persistence**\n\nNow focusing on the code-level implementation for session key persistence. Will utilize the browser's `session` storage to preserve the derived key across refreshes. The key will be exported as raw bytes and then base64-encoded to make the value suitable for storage. The extension can then import the key when needed.\n\nThis approach ensures the vault stays unlocked until the browser closes or the user explicitly locks it. It avoids the need to repeatedly prompt for the master password.\n\n\n**Verifying Session Key Persistence**\n\nImplemented session key persistence using `session` storage. The session key re-imports on page refreshes and tab openings, as designed. The vault now unlocks on browser start or explicit unlock.\n\n**Analyzing Storage Item Race Conditions**\n\nInspecting `saveStoredItem` function to confirm proper item storage in `chrome.storage.local`. This function prevents potential race conditions during save operations ensuring the stored items are saved consistently.\n\n**Confirming Unified Data Storage**\n\nThe issue with separate storage between the local web app and the extension is the core issue. The extension's storage is central. The web app now fully integrates into the extension's `chrome.storage.local`, ensuring a single source of truth for all data.\n\n\n**Implementing Full Dashboard Integration**\n\nThe extension needs a full-page dashboard, accessible from the popup. This dashboard will replicate the localhost web app's functionality, sharing the same storage for unified data access. The dashboard will utilize the existing `chrome.storage.local` and `chrome.storage.session`.\n\n**Adding Import Functionality**\n\nAn import feature will be added to the dashboard, allowing users to import data from the existing localhost web app. This feature will support importing JSON backups or using the master password for data migration. The full dashboard, and the popup settings will receive this feature.\n\n**Enhancing User Navigation**\n\nThe extension's `manifest.json` will be updated to make the dashboard's HTML the options page. The standalone web app will be updated with an export button, simplifying data transfer. This approach ensures easy access to the full dashboard and data portability.\n\n**Refining Domain Matching Logic**\n\nThe `extractDomain` function in `background.js` and `popup.js` requires updating to handle subdomains correctly, such as `vitvellore312.examly.io`. The updated `isDomainMatch` function will check for root domains matching saved URLs or titles for better matching. This will enable domain-based filtering.\n\n\n**Improving Domain Matching Algorithm**\n\nThe existing domain matching logic needed improvement to accurately compare saved domains with current URLs, particularly for subdomains. A refined `isDomainMatch` function was created to handle these cases.\n\nThe updated function normalizes URLs by removing prefixes and suffixes, then performs a subdomain-aware comparison. This enables accurate matches even if the user saves a root domain like `examly.io` and the current URL is a subdomain like `subdomain.examly.io`.\n\n**Verifying Manifest Permissions**\n\nExamining `manifest.json` revealed the required permissions for `storage`, granting access to both `chrome.storage.local` and `chrome.storage.session`. Verified that content scripts can access `chrome.storage.session` via message passing to the background script.\n\nObserved that `chrome.storage.session.setAccessLevel` is unnecessary in the current Manifest V3 setup.\n\n\n**Implementing Full Dashboard Navigation**\n\nThe extension manifest requires updating to specify the full dashboard as the options page, enabling direct access via the extension icon context menu. A \"Full Screen\" button will be added in the popup to launch the full dashboard using an appropriate function.\n\n**Adding Session Key Utilities**\n\nHelper functions will be added to the code to facilitate session key management: `exportRawKey` and `importRawKey` for key serialization and deserialization, and methods for getting, setting, and clearing the session key in `session` storage. These ensure session key persistence and recovery.\n\n**Enhancing Background Script and Popup**\n\nThe background script needs to check for a stored session key upon startup and within every message handler to maintain session key persistence after the service worker's inactivity. The popup should now check for a session key on load and immediately display the dashboard if unlocked, bypassing master password entry. This requires modifying `background.js` and `popup.js`.\n\n**Integrating Full Screen Button**\n\nThe `popup.js` will include the code for a \"Open Full Vault\" button to open the dashboard, and a mechanism to store passwords into local storage (`chrome.storage.local`). The full dashboard will require updates to the `vault.html` and `popup.js` to ensure the full dashboard data and user interactions are synchronized.\n\n**Updating Domain Matching Logic**\n\nThe background script's domain matching logic requires an update for correct handling of subdomains. The implemented subdomain-aware algorithm in the existing code needs to ensure the `isDomainMatch` function provides correct subdomain comparison to match saved URLs with the current website.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"write_to_file","args":{"CodeContent":"/**\n * Cryptographic operations using the browser's native Web Crypto API.\n * PBKDF2 (100,000 iterations, SHA-256) + AES-GCM 256-bit.\n * Zero external dependencies.\n */\n\nexport const CANARY_SECRET = '__OFFLINE_VAULT_CANARY_OK__';\nexport const PBKDF2_ITERATIONS = 100000;\n\nexport function bufferToBase64(buffer) {\n  let binary = '';\n  const bytes = new Uint8Array(buffer);\n  for (let i = 0; i < bytes.byteLength; i++) {\n    binary += String.fromCharCode(bytes[i]);\n  }\n  return btoa(binary);\n}\n\nexport function base64ToBuffer(base64) {\n  const binary = atob(base64);\n  const bytes = new Uint8Array(binary.length);\n  for (let i = 0; i < binary.length; i++) {\n    bytes[i] = binary.charCodeAt(i);\n  }\n  return bytes;\n}\n\nexport function generateSalt(length = 16) {\n  const salt = new Uint8Array(length);\n  crypto.getRandomValues(salt);\n  return bufferToBase64(salt);\n}\n\nexport async function deriveMasterKey(masterPassword, saltBase64) {\n  const encoder = new TextEncoder();\n  const passwordKey = await crypto.subtle.importKey(\n    'raw',\n    encoder.encode(masterPassword),\n    { name: 'PBKDF2' },\n    false,\n    ['deriveKey']\n  );\n\n  const salt = base64ToBuffer(saltBase64);\n\n  return await crypto.subtle.deriveKey(\n    {\n      name: 'PBKDF2',\n      salt,\n      iterations: PBKDF2_ITERATIONS,\n      hash: 'SHA-256'\n    },\n    passwordKey,\n    {\n      name: 'AES-GCM',\n      length: 256\n    },\n    true, // exportable so it can be held in chrome.storage.session during browser session\n    ['encrypt', 'decrypt']\n  );\n}\n\n/**\n * Export CryptoKey to Base64 string for in-memory chrome.storage.session persistence.\n */\nexport async function exportRawKey(key) {\n  const raw = await crypto.subtle.exportKey('raw', key);\n  return bufferToBase64(raw);\n}\n\n/**\n * Re-import CryptoKey from Base64 string stored in chrome.storage.session.\n */\nexport async function importRawKey(keyBase64) {\n  const raw = base64ToBuffer(keyBase64);\n  return await crypto.subtle.importKey(\n    'raw',\n    raw,\n    { name: 'AES-GCM' },\n    true,\n    ['encrypt', 'decrypt']\n  );\n}\n\nexport async function encryptPayload(data, key) {\n  const encoder = new TextEncoder();\n  const iv = new Uint8Array(12);\n  crypto.getRandomValues(iv);\n\n  const encryptedBuffer = await crypto.subtle.encrypt(\n    { name: 'AES-GCM', iv },\n    key,\n    encoder.encode(JSON.stringify(data))\n  );\n\n  return {\n    ciphertext: bufferToBase64(encryptedBuffer),\n    iv: bufferToBase64(iv)\n  };\n}\n\nexport async function decryptPayload(encryptedObj, key) {\n  const ivBuffer = base64ToBuffer(encryptedObj.iv);\n  const dataBuffer = base64ToBuffer(encryptedObj.ciphertext);\n\n  const decryptedBuffer = await crypto.subtle.decrypt(\n    { name: 'AES-GCM', iv: ivBuffer },\n    key,\n    dataBuffer\n  );\n\n  const decoder = new TextDecoder();\n  return JSON.parse(decoder.decode(decryptedBuffer));\n}\n\nexport function generateSecurePassword({\n  length = 20,\n  useLower = true,\n  useUpper = true,\n  useNumbers = true,\n  useSymbols = true,\n  avoidAmbiguous = false\n} = {}) {\n  let lower = 'abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz';\n  let upper = 'ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ';\n  let nums = '0123456789';\n  let syms = '!@#$%^&*()_+-=[]{}|;:,.<>?';\n\n  if (avoidAmbiguous) {\n    lower = lower.replace(/[il1]/g, '');\n    upper = upper.replace(/[IO]/g, '');\n    nums = nums.replace(/[01]/g, '');\n    syms = syms.replace(/[{}[\\]()/\\\\'\"`~,;:.<>]/g, '');\n  }\n\n  let pool = '';\n  const guaranteed = [];\n  const getRand = (max) => {\n    const arr = new Uint32Array(1);\n    crypto.getRandomValues(arr);\n    return arr[0] % max;\n  };\n\n  if (useLower) { pool += lower; guaranteed.push(lower[getRand(lower.length)]); }\n  if (useUpper) { pool += upper; guaranteed.push(upper[getRand(upper.length)]); }\n  if (useNumbers) { pool += nums; guaranteed.push(nums[getRand(nums.length)]); }\n  if (useSymbols) { pool += syms; guaranteed.push(syms[getRand(syms.length)]); }\n  if (!pool) { pool = lower; guaranteed.push(lower[getRand(lower.length)]); }\n\n  const res = [...guaranteed];\n  while (res.length < length) res.push(pool[getRand(pool.length)]);\n\n  for (let i = res.length - 1; i > 0; i--) {\n    const j = getRand(i + 1);\n    [res[i], res[j]] = [res[j], res[i]];\n  }\n\n  return res.join('');\n}\n\nexport function evaluateStrength(pwd) {\n  if (!pwd) return { score: 0, label: 'Empty', color: '#64748b', bits: 0 };\n  let pool = 0;\n  if (/[a-z]/.test(pwd)) pool += 26;\n  if (/[A-Z]/.test(pwd)) pool += 26;\n  if (/[0-9]/.test(pwd)) pool += 10;\n  if (/[^a-zA-Z0-9]/.test(pwd)) pool += 32;\n\n  const bits = Math.floor(pwd.length * Math.log2(pool || 1));\n  if (pwd.length < 8 || bits < 40) return { score: 1, label: 'Weak', color: '#ef4444', bits };\n  if (pwd.length < 12 || bits < 60) return { score: 2, label: 'Fair', color: '#f59e0b', bits };\n  if (pwd.length < 16 || bits < 80) return { score: 3, label: 'Strong', color: '#10b981', bits };\n  return { score: 4, label: 'Very Strong', color: '#34d399', bits };\n}\n","Description":"Add raw key export and import to crypto.js for session persistence","Overwrite":true,"TargetFile":"C:\\Users\\vijay\\.gemini\\antigravity\\scratch\\offline-vault-extension\\crypto.js","toolAction":"Updating crypto.js","toolSummary":"Update crypto.js with session key export/import"}}]}
